<section xml:id="sec-ch04-06-PolarCoordinates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Double Integrals in Polar Coordinates</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      We know that we can label any point in the <m>x y</m>-plane
      (uniquely) in the form ( <m>x, y</m> ). This is what we call
      Cartesian coordinates. In general, there are infinitely different
      sets of coordinates to label points in the plane! We will focus
      specifically on the set of coordinates called polar coordinates.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Polar Coordinates</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Every point <m>(x, y)</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane can be written
        using the polar coordinates <m>(r, \theta)</m>, where:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>r \geq 0</m> is the radius of the circle centered at the
              origin that contains <m>(x, y)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\theta</m> is an angle created from the positive
              <m>x</m>-axis in a counterclockwise motion (for clockwise
              motion, the angle is negative), that gives the direction of
              the vector <m>\langle x, y\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-1">
    <statement>
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-1">
        <caption>The point with Cartesian coordinates ( 4,-3) in Cartesian and Polar Coordinates</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-01. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-01.png">
              <shortdescription>The point with Cartesian coordinates ( 4,-3) in Cartesian and Polar Coordinates</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The point with Cartesian coordinates <m>( 4,-3)</m> in Cartesian and Polar Coordinates</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The point ( <m>4,-3</m> ) in Cartesian and Polar Coordinates
      </p>

      <p>Write the following points in polar coordinates:</p>

      <p>(a) <m>(2,0)</m></p>

      <p>(b) <m>(3,-4)</m></p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The circle, centred at the origin, that passing through the point
        <m>(x,y)= (2,0)</m>, has radius 2. Also, since the point is on the
        positive part of the <m>x</m>-axis the angle is 0. Hence
        <m>(r,\theta ) = (2,0)</m>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The circle, centred at the origin, that passing through the point
        <m>(x,y)= (3,-4)</m>, has radius 5, as much as the distance of that
        point to the origin. Also, from the slope of the line passing
        through the origin and that point we have that
        <m>\tan \theta = -\frac{4}{2}</m>, and since we are in the forth
        quadrant <m>\theta = \arctan \left ( -\frac{4}{3} \right )</m>.
        Hence <m>(r,\theta ) = (5,\arctan \left ( -\frac{4}{3} \right ))</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      For the most part, we will only work with <m>\theta \geq 0</m>; but
      in some cases it will be useful to use negative angles as well.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Formulas to Change from Cartesian to Polar (and vice-versa)</title>

    <p>
      Given a point <m>(x, y)</m> in the plane, you can write it in polar
      coordinates by using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>\tan (\theta)=\frac{y}{x}, x \neq 0</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a point ( <m>r, \theta</m> ) in the plane, you can write it in
      Cartesian coordinates using the formulas:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>x=r \cos (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>y=r \sin (\theta)</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) an iterated integral (using Cartesian
        coordinates) that gives the net volume bounded by the surface
        <m>f(x, y)=x-y^{2}</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane, restricted to the
        disk <m>D</m> of radius 2 centered at the origin.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The disk can be expressed as <m>x^2+y^2\leq 4 </m>. This gives us
        the bounds <m>-\sqrt{4-x^2}\leq y \leq \sqrt{4-x^2}</m> and
        <m>-2\leq x \leq 2</m> for y and x respectively. Hence
        <md>
          \int_{-2}^2 \int_{-\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{\sqrt{4-x^2}} x-y^2 dy dx
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      When the region of integration <m>R</m> is (partly) circular, it
      might be easier to integrate using polar coordinates.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-polar-region">
      <caption>This region is not a circle, but can be easily be described in terms of <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> as <m>R= \{(r, \theta) \mid a \leq r \leq b, \alpha \leq \theta \leq \beta\}</m> (from openstax)</caption>

      <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-03.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>The region between the circle of radius a and radius b, and between angle alpha and angle beta.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>The region between the circle of radius <m>a</m> and radius <m>b</m>, and between angle <m>\alpha</m> and angle <m>\beta</m>.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <note>
    <p>
      To find the net volume bounded by a surface <m>f(x, y)</m> and the
      <m>x y</m>-plane, on a (partly) circular region <m>R</m>, we still
      use rectangular prisms, but now the base is given by a polar
      rectangle.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-polar-rectangles">
      <caption>Partition of a (partly circular) region into polar rectangles (from openstax)</caption>

      <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-04.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Partition of a (partly circular) region into polar rectangles</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>Partition of a (partly circular) region into polar rectangles</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <p>Note that:</p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The base of each rectangular prism is a polar rectangle now, so
          the area of the <m>k</m>-th base isn't just
          <m>\Delta A_{k}=\Delta r_{k} \Delta \theta_{k}</m>. But, after
          some computation, we get that
          <m>\Delta A_{k}=r_{k} \Delta r_{k} \Delta \theta_{k}</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The height of each rectangular prism is still given by the
          function, but we want to write everything in terms of <m>r</m>
          and <m>\theta</m> (instead of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> ). Hence, we
          rewrite <m>f(x, y)</m> as
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
      f(x, y)=f(r, \theta)=f(r \cos (\theta), r \sin (\theta))
    </md>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hence, we can approximate the net volume as
    <md>
      V \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(r_{k} \cos \left(\theta_{k}\right), r_{k} \sin \left(\theta_{k}\right)\right) r_{k} \Delta r_{k} \Delta \theta_{k}
    </md>
    and the net volume is given by
    <md>
      V=\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(r_{k} \cos \left(\theta_{k}\right), r_{k} \sin \left(\theta_{k}\right)\right) r_{k} \Delta r_{k} \Delta \theta_{k}
    </md>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals (Polar Coordinates)</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be continuous on the region <m>R</m> in the
      <m>x y</m>-plane expressed in polar coordinates as
      <m>R=\{(r, \theta) \mid 0 \leq a \leq r \leq b, \alpha \leq \theta \leq \beta\}</m>,
      where <m>|\beta-\alpha| \leq 2 \pi</m> (we don't exceed one rotation).
      Then, we can evaluate the double integral of <m>f</m> over <m>R</m> as
      <md>
        \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{\alpha}^{\beta} \int_{a}^{b} f(r \cos (\theta), r \sin (\theta)) r d r d \theta
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-2-continuation">
    <title>Continuation of Example 19.2</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the iterated integral from
        <xref ref="example-ch04-06-ex-19-2"/> in polar coordinates but don't
        evaluate it.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The disk can now be expressed as <m>r^2\leq 4 </m> and since we
        insist on positive value for r <m>0\leq r \leq 2</m>. Now since
        there are no restrictions for <m>\theta</m> provided in this domain
        <m>0\leq \theta \leq 2\pi</m>. This can also be verified from the
        fact that for choice of a point within our disk, that point can be
        rotated any amount of radiant from its current position and it will
        still be within our disk.
      </p>

      <p>
        Moreover since <m>x=r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y=\sin\theta</m>, then we
        have that <m>f(r,\theta) = r\cos\theta -r^2\sin^2\theta</m>. In
        terms this implies that our integral is
        <md>
          \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 \left( r\cos\theta -r^2\sin^2\theta \right) r drd\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the net volume of the solid bounded by
        <m>f(x, y)=2-\sqrt{9x^{2}+9y^{2}}</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-3">
        <caption>The graph of the cone of the function f(x,y), above the xy-plane.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-06. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-06.png">
              <shortdescription>The graph of the cone of the function f(x,y), above the xy-plane.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The graph of the cone of the function <m>f(x,y)</m>, above the <m>xy</m>-plane.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The (upside-down) cone <m>f(x, y)=2-\sqrt{9x^2+9y^2}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From <m>x=r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y=\sin\theta</m> we see that
        <m>f(r,\theta ) = 2 - \sqrt{9r^2\cos^2\theta +9r^2\sin^2\theta}</m>
        <m> = 2 -\sqrt{9r^2(\cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta )} = 2 - \sqrt{9r^2} = 2 - 3r</m>
        since <m>r\geq 0 </m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the domain of integration is the set of point for which
        <m>f(x,y)\geq 0</m> from the fact that the cone is above the
        xy-plane (Image or the fact that it is of the form
        <m>c-\sqrt{\text{quadratic expression}}</m> and hence it faces
        downwards). That implies that <m>2-\sqrt{9x^{2}+9y^{2}}\geq 0</m> or
        in similar spirit with the previous calculations
        <m>2-3r \geq 0 \Rightarrow r\leq \frac{2}{3}</m>. Since again we
        have no restrictions on the domain for <m>\theta</m>, we use
        <m>0\leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m> for the bounds.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\frac{2}{3} \left( 2-3r\right) r dr d\theta  =\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\frac{2}{3}  2r-3r^2 dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \left . r^2-r^3\right |_0^{\frac{2}{3}}d\theta</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_0^{2\pi} \left( \frac{4}{9}- \frac{8}{27}\right) d\theta = \left( \frac{4}{9}- \frac{8}{27}\right) \left . \theta  \right |_0^{2\pi} = \frac{8\pi}{27}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the net volume of the solid bounded by the paraboloids
        <m>z=x^{2}+y^{2}</m> and <m>z=12-2x^{2}-2y^{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-4">
        <caption>The graph of the bottom part of the paraboloid z equals 12 minus 2 x squared minus 2 y squared, meeting the upper part of the paraboloid z equals x squared plus y squared.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-07. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-07.png">
              <shortdescription>The graph of the bottom part of the paraboloid z equals 12 minus 2 x squared minus 2 y squared, meeting the upper part of the paraboloid z equals x squared plus y squared.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The graph of the bottom part of the paraboloid <m>z=12-2x^2-2y^2</m>, meeting the upper part of the paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The volume bounded by the paraboloids
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x,y)=12-2x^{2}-2y^{2}</m> and <m>g(x,y)= x^{2}+y^{2}</m>.
        They intersect at
        <m>f(x,y)=g(x,y)\Rightarrow 12-2x^{2}-2y^{2} = x^{2}+y^{2} \Rightarrow x^2+y^2 =4</m>
        and <m>f(x,y)</m> is above <m>g(x,y)</m> within that bounded domain
        <m>x^2+y^2\leq 4</m>. (Image or compare their value at
        <m>(0,0)</m>)
      </p>

      <p>
        From <m>x=r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y=\sin\theta</m> we see that
        <m>f(r,\theta ) = 12 - 2r^2\cos^2\theta -2r^2\sin^2\theta</m>
        <m> = 12 - 2r^2\left( \cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta\right) = 12 - 2r^2</m>.
        Also
        <m>g(r,\theta) = r^cos^2\theta +r^2\sin^2\theta = r^2\left ( \cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta \right) = r^2</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the domain of integration is the set of points
        <m>x^2+y^2 \leq 4</m> or in Polar coordinates <m>0\leq r \leq 2</m>.
        Since again we have no restrictions on the domain for
        <m>\theta</m>, we use <m>0\leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m> for the bounds.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^2 \left( f(r,\theta ) - g(r,\theta )\right) r dr d\theta  =\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^2 (12-3r^2)r dr d\theta =\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^2 12r-3r^3 dr d\theta</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_0^{2\pi} \left . 6r^2-\frac{3}{4}r^4\right |_0^{2}d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} 24-12 d\theta = 12 \left . \theta  \right |_0^{2\pi} =24 \pi</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Integrating Over General Polar Regions</title>

    <p>
      We can also use polar coordinates when a region is bounded by
      <m>r=g(\theta)</m> and <m>r=h(\theta)</m> (instead of just constant
      values for the radius <m>r</m> ).
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-general-polar-region">
      <caption>(Partly) circular region bounded by <m>r=h(\theta)</m> and <m>r=g(\theta)</m> (from openstax)</caption>

      <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-08.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>A partly circular region bounded by r equals h of theta and r equals g of theta, between the angles of theta from alpha to beta.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>A partly circular region bounded by <m>r=h(\theta)</m> and <m>r=g(\theta)</m>, between the angles of <m>\theta</m> from <m>\alpha</m> to <m>\beta</m>.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals (Polar Coordinates)</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be continuous on the region <m>R</m> in the
      <m>x y</m>-plane expressed in polar coordinates as
      <m>R=\{(r, \theta) \mid 0 \leq g(\theta) \leq r \leq h(\theta), \alpha \leq \theta \leq \beta\}</m>,
      where <m>|\beta-\alpha| \leq 2 \pi</m> (we don't exceed one rotation).
      Then, we can evaluate the double integral of <m>f</m> over <m>R</m> as
      <md>
        \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{\alpha}^{\beta} \int_{g(\theta)}^{h(\theta)} f(r \cos (\theta), r \sin (\theta)) r d r d \theta
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      To find the bounds <m>r=g(\theta)</m> and <m>r=h(\theta)</m> when
      integrating using polar coordinates:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Draw a sketch of the region <m>R</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Starting from the origin <m>(0,0)</m> draw a typical radius
            towards the region <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the radius intersects first (as it enters the
            region) is the lower bound <m>r=g(\theta)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Whichever boundary the radius intersects second (as it exits the
            region) is the upper bound <m>r=h(\theta)</m> of the region.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area of the region <m>R</m> in the first quadrant above
        <m>y=\frac{1}{2}</m> and below <m>x^{2}+y^{2}=1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-5">
        <caption>The area in the first quadrant bounded by y equals one half and x squared plus y squared equals 1.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-09. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-09.png">
              <shortdescription>The area in the first quadrant bounded by y equals one half and x squared plus y squared equals 1.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The area in the first quadrant bounded by <m>y=\frac{1}{2}</m> and <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From <m>x=r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y=\sin\theta</m> we see that
        <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> we receive <m>r=1</m>. Also <m>y=\frac{1}{2}</m>
        implies <m>r\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2}</m> or
        <m>r=\frac{1}{2\sin\theta}=\frac{1}{2}\csc\theta</m>. The above to
        lines intersect at
        <m>1\cdot\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{\pi}{6}</m>
        or <m>\theta = \frac{5\pi}{6}</m>. Since we are restricted to the
        first quadrant we only take into account
        <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{6}</m>. Lastly, from the fact that we
        restrict ourselves on the first quadrant, the left most boundary
        line of this region is the <m>y</m>-axis or <m>x=0</m> or
        <m>r\cos\theta = 0 \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> or
        <m> \theta = \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>. As previously we only take into
        account <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        To find the area we would normally evaluate
        <m>\iint_R 1 dx dy</m> or <m>\iint_R 1 dy dx</m>, in other words
        the function that we integrate is <m>f(x,y)=1</m>. That implies that
        <m>f(r, \theta )=1</m> and that the desired integral is
        <m>\iint_R 1 r dr d\theta</m>.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_\frac{\pi}{6}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\int_{\frac{1}{2}\csc\theta}^1  r dr d\theta = \int_\frac{\pi}{6}^{\frac{\pi}{2}} \left . \frac{1}{2} r^2\right |_{\frac{1}{2}\csc\theta}^1 d\theta  = \int_\frac{\pi}{6}^{\frac{\pi}{2}} \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{8}\csc^2\theta d\theta =  \left . \frac{\theta}{2} +\frac{1}{8}\cot\theta \right |_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^\frac{\pi}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{\pi}{12} +0 - \frac{\sqrt 3}{8} = \frac{\pi}{6}- \frac{\sqrt 3}{8}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      In the next example, it will be useful to use negative angles. But be
      careful: If <m>\theta=\alpha</m> and <m>\theta=\beta</m> are the lower
      and upper <m>\theta</m>-bounds for a region of integration, then as
      numbers, it must be that <m>\alpha \leq \beta</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) an iterated integral that gives the
        area of the region outside the circle <m>r=2</m> and inside the
        circle <m>r=4 \cos (\theta)</m>, the latter for
        <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, as pictured
        below.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-6">
        <caption>The area of the region outside the circle r equals 2 and inside the circle r equals 4 cosine of theta.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-11. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-6" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-11.png">
              <shortdescription>The area of the region outside the circle r equals 2 and inside the circle r equals 4 cosine of theta.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The area of the region outside the circle <m>r=2</m> and inside the circle <m>r=4\cos(\theta)</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <m>r=2</m> in orange and <m>r=4 \cos (\theta)</m> in blue
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since we want outside the circle <m>r=2</m> we immediately have the
        lower bound for <m>r</m> being 2. Since we want inside the circle
        <m>r=4\cos\theta</m> we have the upper bound for <m>r</m> being
        <m>4\cos\theta</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now in order to find the bounds for <m>\theta</m> we need find the
        points of intersection. If  <m>r=2</m> and <m>r=4\cos\theta</m>
        concurrently, that implies that
        <m>2= 4 \cos\theta \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{1}{2} \Rightarrow \theta = \pm \frac{\pi}{3}</m>.
        To determine if we integrate from <m>-\frac{\pi}{3}</m> to
        <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> or  from <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> to
        <m>-\frac{\pi}{3}</m> and  <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> to
        <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, we either consult the image or we test on
        point in each range. For <m>\theta = 0</m> we have that for the
        second circle we are at the point
        <m>(r,\theta )=(4\cos 0 , 0) = (4,0)</m> and hence outside the
        circle <m>r=2</m>. Similarly,  for <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> we
        have that for the second circle we are at the point
        <m>(r,\theta )=(4\cos \frac{\pi}{2} , 0) = (0,0)</m> and hence
        inside the circle <m>r=2</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence the desired integral is
        <md>
          \int_{-\frac{\pi}{3}}^{\frac{\pi}{3}}\int_2^{4\cos\theta} r dr d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Partitioning Regions of Integration</title>

    <p>
      If at any point, as <m>\theta</m> sweeps through the region of
      integration <m>R</m>, the lower and upper bounds <m>r=g(\theta)</m>
      and <m>r=h(\theta)</m> change, you need to set up multiple iterated
      integrals.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-06-ex-19-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) an iterated integral(s) that gives the
        area of the region in the first and fourth quadrant bounded by the
        circle <m>r=4</m>, the <m>y</m>-axis and <m>x=2</m> , as shown
        below:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-06-ex-19-7">
        <caption>The area of the region in the first and fourth quadrant bounded by the circle with radius 4, the y-axis and x equals 2.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-13. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-06-ex-19-7" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_17659320b7406a9d64bfg-13.png">
              <shortdescription>The area of the region in the first and fourth quadrant bounded by the circle with radius 4, the y-axis and x equals 2.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The area of the region in the first and fourth quadrant bounded by the circle with radius 4, the <m>y</m>-axis and <m>x=2</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The problem here arises from the fact that there not a single
        integral in the order of integration <m>drd\theta</m> that can
        express the area of the the above shape. The issue stems from the
        fact that for some part of the area the farthest away from the
        origin, radiating outwards, that we can get is the circle
        <m>r=4</m> and for other parts the line <m>x=2</m>. We will need as
        many integrals then as the intersection points of the above, plus
        one. One integral before the first intersection point, one in
        between the first and the seconds, the second and the third, and so
        on until the integral in between the last intersection point. The
        first and the last integral in this list may be ignored depending on
        the region (they may be equal to 0, since the start/end of the
        region of the integration is an intersection point as with the
        previous example).
      </p>

      <p>
        The <m>y</m>-axis provides the bound <m>x=0</m> which transforms to
        <m>r\cos\theta= 0 \Rightarrow \theta = \pm \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. The
        line <m>x=2</m> forces us into the forth and first quadrant so we
        will traverse from <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> to <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
        Also, at any place of this region we can contract radially to the
        origin, hence our lower bound for the variable <m>r</m> is 0. Now
        for the upper bounds. The line <m>x=2</m> is expressed in polar
        coordinates as the equation <m>r\cos \theta =2 </m> or
        <m>r=\frac{2}{\cos \theta } </m>. The intersection of
        <m>r\cos \theta =2 </m> and <m>r=4</m> comes from the equation
        <m>4\cos\theta = 2 \Rightarrow  \theta = \pm \frac{\pi}{3} </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Between <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>-\frac{\pi}{3}</m> the points
        of the circle <m>r =4</m> are closer to the origin (lower bound)
        than the point of the line <m>r=\frac{2}{\cos \theta } </m>, we can
        test with a value, say <m>\theta= -\frac{9\pi}{20}</m> which gives
        us the point
        <m>(r,\theta) = \left(\frac{2}{\cos (-\frac{9\pi}{20}) },\frac{9\pi}{20}\right) \approx \left(12.78,-\frac{9\pi}{20}\right)</m>.
        This implies that the upper bound of the integral is <m>r=4</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        Between <m>-\frac{\pi}{3}</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> the points of
        the circle <m>r =4</m> are farther from the origin (lower bound)
        than the point of the line <m>r=\frac{2}{\cos \theta } </m>, we can
        test with a value, say <m>\theta= 0</m> which gives us the point
        <m>(r,\theta) = \left(\frac{2}{\cos (0) },0\right) = (2,0)</m>. This
        implies that the upper bound of the integral is
        <m>r=\frac{2}{\cos \theta }</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        Between <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> the points of
        the circle <m>r =4</m> are closer to the origin (lower bound) than
        the point of the line <m>r=\frac{2}{\cos \theta } </m>, we can test
        with a value, say <m>\theta= \frac{9\pi}{20}</m> which gives us the
        point
        <m>(r,\theta) = \left(\frac{2}{\cos (\frac{9\pi}{20}) },\frac{9\pi}{20}\right) \approx \left(12.78,\frac{9\pi}{20}\right)</m>.
        This implies that the upper bound of the integral is <m>r=4</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence the volume can be express as the sum of the following
        integrals
        <md>
          \int_{-\frac{\pi}{2}}^{-\frac{\pi}{3}}\int_0^{4} r dr d\theta+\int_{-\frac{\pi}{3}}^{\frac{\pi}{3}}\int_0^{\frac{2}{\cos\theta}} r dr d\theta+ \int_{\frac{\pi}{3}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\int_0^{4} r dr d\theta
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
